  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 346 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nEarly this morning, a rather nebulous pattern continues over the \nregion with a weak cutoff upper low over western Texas, a modestly \nstrong deep layer trough over the northern plains, and a weak \nmid/upper ridge axis extending from the eastern Pacific to the \ncentral Rockies. At the surface, a weak frontal boundary extends \nfrom the Dakotas to eastern Colorado, and a weak surface ridge\nresides over the Ozarks and the lower Mississippi River Valley.\nShort range models over the past 24 hours have been fairly \nconsistent with the potential of patchy fog across portions of \nsouthern and southeast Kansas this morning; however, this has \nclearly not materialized which makes sense given modestly high \ndewpoint depressions persisting across these areas. Still, \npatchy fog is possible this morning along and south of US-54, \nand any fog that develops should quickly dissipate after \nsunrise. Sunny skies will allow for ample diurnal heating, and \nthis will promote afternoon highs in the low to mid 70s. The \naforementioned frontal boundary will sag into central Kansas \ntonight, but lows will still only bottom out in the 40s. This \nfrontal boundary will bring slightly cooler air to central \nKansas on Tuesday with highs in the mid 60s; however, southern \nand southeast Kansas will continue to see afternoon temperatures\nin the low to mid 70s. The frontal boundary will wash out, and \nsoutherly flow will return Tuesday night allowing for a milder \nairmass to slide into the region. Therefore, lows will only dip \ninto the mid 40s to low 50s Tuesday night.\n\nMid range guidance remains quite consistent with showing deep \nlayer troughing across the Great Lakes region from mid to late \nweek. This will leave the door open to a series of frontal \npassages that will bring a modest cool down to the region during\nthe latter half of the week. Persistent northerly winds will \nbegin on Wednesday with afternoon highs in the 60s, then \nafternoon highs Thursday and Friday will only top out in the 50s\nwith Friday being the cooler of the 2 days. Additionally, it \nappears increasingly likely that much of the region will \nexperience another freeze Thursday night into Friday morning. By\nthe weekend, mid/upper ridging returns to the central plains \nalong with southerly low level flow promoting a gradual warm up \nthrough Monday.",
